## Purpose

Make App Server the dependable first opportunity for eligible Tool Shed work after explicit owner
opt-in. Preserve safe GUI fallback, keep clean installs off, retain consent across ordinary upgrades,
and produce trustworthy local evidence about opportunities, executions, failures, and reconciliation.

## Desired Outcome

The protected preference remains the sole active authority and survives normal upgrades. An explicit
owner-profile recovery copy outside Codex home can restore intent after destructive replacement but
never acts as live consent by itself. Runtime events are versioned, privacy-bounded, correlated, and
isolated from validator traffic. A concise local report distinguishes opportunities, selections,
executions, completion, GUI fallback, reconciliation, controlled failures, and skipped opportunities.
The implementation is qualified on supported platforms, upgraded in both maintainer and installed
clients, dogfooded once with explicit consent, released, and reconciled back to IDEA-0010.

## First Useful Boundary

- Keep the existing protected preference as active authority and add explicit owner-profile recovery.
- Isolate default preference and event paths through `TOOL_SHED_STATE_ROOT` during validation.
- Add schema-v2 source, event-type, role, and correlation fields without rewriting schema-v1 history.
- Add a bounded aggregate report and prove safe pre-mutation fallback and post-mutation reconciliation.
- Update the configured Work2 target and register the candidate without pushing or releasing.

